diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — VLAN 20 (172.16.20.0/24) unable to reach VLAN 10 (172.16.10.0/24) — does not exist in the live lab. Direct probing disproves each hypothesis raised in the ticket.

Router0's inter-VLAN routing is fully operational: both sub-interfaces eth0.10 (172.16.10.1/24) and eth0.20 (172.16.20.1/24) are administratively and operationally UP, IPv4 forwarding is enabled (ip_forward=1), and the kernel routing table contains directly connected entries for both subnets. There are zero iptables or nftables rules on router0 that could block traffic in either direction — all chains are in default ACCEPT policy with no entries.

The Layer-2 path is equally clean. Switch1 correctly places laptop0's access port (eth7) into the br_vlan20 bridge and pc4's access port (eth1) into the br_vlan10 bridge, with VLAN-tagged trunk sub-interfaces (eth0.10/eth0.20) carrying inter-VLAN frames up to mswitch1 and on to router0. Mswitch1 has no ebtables rules. End-to-end pings in both directions — laptop0 (172.16.20.10) to pc4 (172.16.10.4) and back — complete with 0% packet loss and TTL=63, confirming exactly one router hop through router0 as designed.

Root cause: There is no fault. The ticket describes a non-existent outage; the inter-VLAN routing infrastructure between VLAN 20 and VLAN 10 is correctly configured and fully functional in both directions.

Corrective action: No change required. The on-call engineer should close the ticket as a false alarm and confirm with the user that connectivity is working; the issue may have been transient or was a misdiagnosis.
=== END ===
